 <p class="lede"><Term>Situational awareness</Term> is your perception and understanding of what is actually happening around you. No single sensor gives it: you assemble it from the radar, the warning receiver, your flight, the controller, support assets, your eyes and mutual support. <a href="#source-mch">{m('11')}</a> Fighter pilots are not born with it; it has to be developed and kept current, and the aim is the big picture, with no surprises. <a href="#source-mch">{m('11')}</a></p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to:</p>
 <ol class="outcomes">
  <li>set task priorities that protect aircraft control, terrain, other aircraft, fuel and threats before anything else;</li>
  <li>run a <Term>time-sharing</Term> lookout and drop and rebuild sectors in the right order;</li>
  <li>describe a <Term>mutual support</Term> contract and how the flight and the controller share one picture;</li>
  <li>use <Term id="Forward-looking infrared">FLIR</Term>, targeting pods and an <Term>image intensifier</Term> within what each can see;</li>
  <li>say what a <Term>tactical data link</Term> such as <Term>Link 16</Term> adds and what it does not;</li>
  <li>fuse every source into one picture, <Term id="Radar correlation">correlation</Term> first;</li>
  <li>use <Term>IFF</Term> to identify friends, and say what a missing reply does and does not tell you.</li>
 </ol>
 <p class="note">These are principles that apply in any cockpit, taken from an Air Force fighter handbook, a Navy intercept training text and joint doctrine. Their examples are fighters; the same priorities, lookout and fusion apply in helicopters and multi-crew aircraft, whose crew coordination standards are their own. The worked picture is invented. How the radar and warning receiver work is in <a href={radar}>Radar, Warning Receivers and Countermeasures</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="example">One picture, built and kept</h2>
 <p>Here is one invented two-ship sortie, from the brief to a decision, showing where each piece of the picture comes from. The sequence and calls follow the training texts; the tracks are made up. Figure 1 shows the picture with each source switched on or off.</p>
 <ol class="exchange script">
  <li><span class="who">Before flight</span> <span class="said">Chair-fly the sortie: where will you be busiest, what comes first then, and what you would do at joker fuel. <a class="step" href="#priorities">Why</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Your eyes</span> <span class="said">Wingman in sight, line abreast; near rocks, far rocks, check six, then the wider sectors. <a class="step" href="#lookout">Why</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Controller</span> <span class="said">"Single group, bullseye 350/60, 25 thousand, hostile." <a class="step" href="#mutual">Why</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Datalink</span> <span class="said">Shows that hostile track, a friendly flight to the northwest, a surface threat from the brief, and an unknown track to the east no one has identified. <a class="step" href="#datalink">Why</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Radar</span> <span class="said">A contact at 354°, 38 NM, 25,000 ft, hot: it matches the call. You call "contact" with its position and "declare", and the controller confirms hostile. <a class="step" href="#fusion">Why</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Interrogator</span> <span class="said">A Mode 4 challenge across the volume ahead: the friendly flight replies correctly; the group north and the track east give no reply. That alone makes neither of them hostile. Your wingman, on the beam, is outside it; you hold him by eye and datalink. <a class="step" href="#iff">Why</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Warning receiver</span> <span class="said">An airborne radar in search near 12 o'clock, a bearing that fits the group; a surface radar at 3 o'clock where the briefed threat is. <a class="step" href="#fusion">Why</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">You</span> <span class="said">One hostile group, correlated by four sources; one unknown, held by the datalink and silent to IFF, kept in the picture as unknown and asked about. <a class="step" href="#fusion">Why</a></span></li>
 </ol>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="picture" aria-labelledby="picture-title">
  <h3 id="picture-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — Where the picture comes from</h3>
  <p>Switch each source off and on. A track shown with several sources is correlated; a warning receiver alone gives only a bearing.</p>
  <div data-sources></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 360" role="img" aria-label="Plan view centered on your aircraft with range rings, your wingman, a friendly flight to the northwest that answers IFF, a hostile group to the north, an unknown track to the east and a surface threat to the east, each shown according to the sources switched on"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. The friendly flight to the northwest is held by the datalink and identified by its Mode 4 reply. The hostile group to the north is held by the radar, the controller, the datalink and the warning receiver, and gives no IFF reply; the unknown track to the east only by the datalink, and it gives no IFF reply; the surface threat by the warning receiver, as a bearing, and by the datalink from the brief.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">An invented picture, ranges to scale (rings every 10 NM). The call formats follow the training instruction. <a href="#source-p825">{p('5-9, 10-3')}</a></p>
 </section>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> each source gives part of the picture; the picture you can act on is the one several sources agree on.</p>

 <h2 id="priorities">Priorities and what SA is</h2>
 <p>In any busy mission there are moments when you cannot do everything. The handbook's answer is a have-to-do list, set long before you reach the aircraft, with nice-to-do tasks such as answering fuel checks or passing a weather report below it. <a href="#source-mch">{m('8 to 9')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Top priority tasks" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>The tasks that never go away <a href="#source-mch">{m('8 to 9')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Priority</th><th scope="col">Task</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Maintain aircraft control</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Never hit the ground, or anything attached to it</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Never hit anything in the air, such as your lead or wingman</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Never run out of fuel</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">5</th><td>Never let anything shot from the ground or air hit your aircraft</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The order can shift: in close formation in cloud at 20,000 ft, avoiding your flight matters more than the ground. Letting nice-to-do tasks take priority is misprioritization, and the handbook says many of its own revisions came from accidents it caused. <a href="#source-mch">{m('9')}</a> Mission objectives built around the flight's lowest common denominator, and chair-flying the critical moments before you strap in, delay task saturation. <a href="#source-mch">{m('10 to 11')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> SA rests on priorities set before flight: control, terrain, other aircraft, fuel and threats first, everything else after.</p>

 <h2 id="lookout">Lookout and the cross-check</h2>
 <p>Visual lookout is a priority task for every flight member, leads as well as wingmen. The handbook's reason: historically 90% of air-to-air kills came from attacks the victim did not see. <a href="#source-mch">{m('20')}</a> You can do only one task at a time, so lookout is time-shared: the airspace is divided into sectors, each given a priority. <a href="#source-mch">{m('23')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Lookout sectors" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>The time-share plan, highest priority first (number two in a low-level formation) <a href="#source-mch">{m('23 to 24')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Sector</th><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">What it covers</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Near rocks</td><td>The ground that will affect your flight path in the next 10 to 15 seconds: the hub of the cross-check</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">1A</th><td>Far rocks</td><td>The terrain that will affect your future maneuvering</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Check six</td><td>Inside the flight, behind: your position and your lead's six o'clock</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Inside the flight, ahead</td><td>Ahead of the 3/9 line: bandits converting, surface-to-air missiles from the front</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Outside the flight</td><td>Ahead of and behind the 3/9 line, outside the formation: the lowest priority</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Start every mission with the basic pattern, near rocks, far rocks, check six, and build out as workload allows. When it rises, in rough terrain, defensive reactions or navigation turns, drop the lowest sectors in order; in hard turns only near rocks may be left. Rebuild one sector at a time. <a href="#source-mch">{m('22 to 23')}</a> The radar has a place too: leads fold it into sector 3, wingmen look at it after sector 4, and a switch change replaces a sector search, never the near rocks. <a href="#source-mch">{m('24')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="lookout" aria-labelledby="lookout-title">
  <h3 id="lookout-title" class="fig-title">Figure 2 — Time-sharing the lookout</h3>
  <p>Set your position and the workload; the highlighted sectors are the ones still in your cross-check, numbered in order.</p>
  <div class="controls">
   <label for="lookout-position">Your position</label><select id="lookout-position" name="position"><option value="wing">Wingman (two or four)</option><option value="lead">Lead or number three</option></select>
   <label for="lookout-load">Workload</label><select id="lookout-load" name="load"></select>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 2, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 360" role="img" aria-label="Plan view of a two-ship with the lookout sectors drawn round the wingman: near and far rocks ahead, the flight's six behind, inside the flight ahead, and outside the flight to either side"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. With a light load the cross-check runs sectors 1, 1A, 2, 3 and 4, with the radar placed by position; busy, it keeps 1, 1A and 2; in a hard turn only sector 1, the near rocks.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Schematic sector shapes after the handbook's time-share plan, not to scale. How many sectors each workload keeps is this figure's illustration of the rule to drop the lowest first. <a href="#source-mch">{m('22 to 24')}</a></p>
 </section>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> near rocks, far rocks and check six come first; under load drop the lowest sectors in order and rebuild one at a time.</p>

 <h2 id="mutual">Mutual support and the team</h2>
 <p>Mutual support is a subset of SA: a contract within a flight of two or more aircraft. It demands knowing where your flight and the threat are, and what both sides' weapons can do; the flight lead sets it to the experience of the flight. <a href="#source-mch">{m('19')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="A mutual support contract" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>What a sound mutual support contract provides <a href="#source-mch">{m('19')}</a></caption>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Position awareness</th><td>Of the other flight members</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Early threat awareness</th><td>Of the threat and its attack axis</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Communication</th><td>Of offensive and defensive information to the flight</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Targeting</th><td>Weapons employment before the threat attacks</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Options</th><td>The ability to press the attack or disengage</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Visual formations give all of this, and the time you spend holding position affects how well you can see a threat by eye, radar or controller. <a href="#source-mch">{m('20')}</a> The controller is part of the team too: fighter and controller build a common understanding of the picture, so brief and debrief together. When you ask for a picture, the controller gives every known hostile, bandit or bogey group; a group you hold that the controller has not called, you call and ask to have declared. <a href="#source-p825">{p('5-11')}</a> Formation flying itself is in <a href={formation}>Formation Flying</a>.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> mutual support is a briefed contract: know where your flight and the threat are, tell each other, and keep the option to press or leave.</p>

 <h2 id="eoir">Infrared, pods and night vision</h2>
 <p>Two kinds of imaging sensor extend your eyes, and neither is better: each wins in some conditions. <a href="#source-usna">{u('10.5')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Imaging sensors compared" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Image intensifiers and FLIR <a href="#source-usna">{u('10.5.3')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col"><Term>Image intensifier</Term> (night vision goggles, low-light TV)</th><th scope="col"><Term id="Forward-looking infrared">FLIR</Term></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Sees</th><td>Faint visible light, amplified 1,000 to 10,000 times</td><td>Heat contrast; no visible light needed</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Needs</th><td>Some light from stars, moon or sky; cannot see in complete darkness</td><td>Thermal contrast; best at night, when warm objects stand out against a cool background</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Limits</th><td>Considerable loss of detail</td><td>Cloud, rain and humidity absorb infrared, so range is less than radar's; sees through light fog and haze better than the eye</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Goggles also narrow your view: a common goggle has a 40° field of view, against about 190° for both eyes unaided, so you must move your head to scan. <a href="#source-wiki-nvd">[6, Night-vision device]</a> A targeting pod carries a FLIR and usually a TV camera on a stabilized turret; the records in <a href={pods}>Radar &amp; Sensors</a> cover the <Term record="litening">LITENING</Term>, <Term record="sniper">Sniper</Term> and <Term record="atflir">ATFLIR</Term> pods, and using one to find and mark a target is in <a href={targeting}>Precision Targeting</a>.</p>
 <p>Use the picture without letting it take you. The handbook allows a FLIR picture in the head-up display as another horizon reference and to see cloud on recovery at night, but warns that too bright an image hides the normal symbology and can reflect on the canopy, and that a wingman rejoining should fly the rejoin, not look at the pod display. <a href="#source-mch">{m('284')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> goggles need some light and narrow your view; a FLIR needs heat contrast and loses range in moisture; neither is a reason to stop flying the aircraft.</p>

 <h2 id="datalink">Datalinks and the shared picture</h2>
 <p>A tactical data link exchanges tracks and other data by digital radio between aircraft, ships, ground units and control agencies. In joint air defense, a link such as Link 16 shares the <Term>common tactical picture</Term> between the services' systems, and track information from each component is consolidated over data link interfaces by assigned track managers. <a href="#source-jp">{j('II-12, II-26')}</a> The older links show the idea: Link 11 relays sensor information and engagement status, and Link 4 lets a controller pass target data to a fighter and the fighter's data back. <a href="#source-usna">{u('20.4.4')}</a></p>
 <p>Link 16 itself is a secure, jam-resistant, time-division link on 960 to 1,215 MHz, carrying standard J-series messages such as precise participant location and identification. <a href="#source-wiki-l16">[6, Link 16]</a> In the cockpit that means seeing friendly aircraft and tracks your own sensors do not hold, and the controller's display can combine its radar with linked data down to fuel state and weapon load. <a href="#source-p825">{p('5-5')}</a></p>
 <p>Limits follow from how it works. The picture is only as good as its track management: one formation can be shown as a single track with a strength field, to save workload and messages. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-15')}</a> A linked track is someone else's sensor at an earlier moment, not your own.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> a datalink lets you see what others see, in a common picture; it does not replace correlating what you see yourself.</p>

 <h2 id="iff">IFF: identifying friends</h2>
 <p><Term>IFF</Term> is a challenge and reply. An <Term>interrogator</Term> transmits a coded challenge; the <Term>transponder</Term> in a friendly aircraft answers it automatically, and the interrogator evaluates the reply. <a href="#source-ewf">{e('A-21, A-23, A-45')}</a> The <Term>selective identification feature</Term> lets the reply carry a selected code, so the answer can say more than "friendly". <a href="#source-ewf">{e('A-39')}</a> Interrogators sit in fighters and in the controllers' radars: a control radar's display shows the raw radar return, the primary target, and its IFF reply, the secondary target, and can read IFF beyond 200 NM. <a href="#source-p825">{p('5-1, 5-5')}</a></p>
 <ReferenceTable record="radio-and-visual-signals" table="iff-modes" />
 <p>Which modes and codes are in use is set for each operation in the air tasking order and special instructions. <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-3')}</a> In joint doctrine's sample identification matrix, a correct Mode 4 reply, or a datalink position-and-identity report (<Term>Link 16</Term>'s PPLI), makes a track positive friendly; a valid Mode 1 or 2 only leads on to a procedural check of whether the track is on a minimum-risk route; a track without one is checked against air routes with Mode 3/C, the minimum-risk routes and its flight profile. <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-5')}</a> In the air you set the IFF as assigned at the fence check, a controller can give the wingman a separate code, and at the fence-out the wingman sets the IFF to standby. <a href="#source-p825">{p('3-10, 3-19 to 3-20')}</a> Where IFF is switched on or off is planned in the airspace control plan along with the minimum-risk routes and the areas for sanitizing returning packages from enemy aircraft. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-8')}</a> The calls are brevity: <Term>SQUAWK</Term> to operate the IFF as indicated, <Term>STRANGLE</Term> to turn it off, and <Term>PARROT</Term> for the transponder itself. <a href="#source-p825">{p('A-7, A-9 to A-10')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="What an IFF result tells you" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>What an interrogation tells you</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">What it means</th><th scope="col">What it does not mean</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Correct encrypted reply (Mode 4)</th><td>A positive identification as friendly <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-3, A-5')}</a></td><td>That every system shows it on the right track: modes and codes can be swapped between tracks in a shared picture <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-8')}</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Valid Mode 1 or 2</th><td>A start for procedural identification: whether it is on a minimum-risk route <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-5')}</a></td><td>A positive friendly</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">No valid Mode 1 or 2</th><td>Procedural checks: air route and Mode 3/C, minimum-risk route and flight profile, which can end in neutral, unknown or hostile <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-5')}</a></td><td>Hostile on the reply alone</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">No reply, or a wrong one</th><td>Not identified as a friend</td><td>Hostile: friendly aircraft without IFF transponders can share the airspace <a href="#source-jp">{j('D-7')}</a>, and a positive identification is more than a lack of friend <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-13, A-3')}</a></td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Most positive hostile identifications come from technology that exploits the target's own physical or electronic signature, such as non-cooperative target recognition, signals intelligence or electronic support; visual identification is also a positive method. <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-3 to A-4')}</a> Procedural identification can also end in hostile, for a track that breaks the airspace rules and is not otherwise identified. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-13')}</a> That is why <Term>positive identification</Term> lists IFF beside those methods, not above them. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-13')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> IFF can positively identify a friend; no reply leaves a track unknown, never hostile on its own.</p>

 <h2 id="fusion">Fusing the sources</h2>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="What each source gives" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>What each source contributes</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Gives</th><th scope="col">Does not give</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Your radar</th><td>Bearing, range, altitude and closure of contacts in its volume</td><td>Identity on its own; anything outside its volume or in its notch <a href={radar}>(radar module)</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Warning receiver</th><td>Which radars, roughly where, and what mode <a href="#source-ewf">{e('17-8 to 17-10')}</a></td><td>Range or a fix; passive infrared threats <a href="#source-ewf">{e('15-6, 17-1')}</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Controller</th><td>The wider picture in bullseye or BRAA, and declarations <a href="#source-p825">{p('5-7 to 5-9')}</a></td><td>Close control at long range from a slow-scanning radar <a href="#source-p825">{p('5-1')}</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">IFF interrogator</th><td>Friends, by a correct encrypted reply <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-5')}</a></td><td>A hostile identity: no reply is not a foe <a href="#source-jp">{j('D-7')}</a></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Datalink</th><td>Other units' tracks and identities in the common picture <a href="#source-jp">{j('II-12')}</a></td><td>Your own confirmation</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Eyes, goggles, FLIR and pods</th><td>Visual and thermal pictures; a visual check of what a track is</td><td>Range and identity at long range</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Fusion starts with correlation: placing your contact against the controller's call in bearing, range and altitude. If you see nothing where the group is called, put the cursor there and adjust the volume; once you have the contact, give its position and ask for a declaration, and the controller repeats it with the identity. <a href="#source-p825">{p('10-3')}</a></p>
 <p>Identification works the same way at joint level: information from onboard, off-board, air, surface and space sensors, gathered by reliable voice and data networks, supports the engagement decision, and a friendly can be identified by voice or datalink. <a href="#source-jp">{j('A-1, A-5')}</a> A track only one source holds stays in the picture as what it is: unknown until someone identifies it.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> correlate before you act: a contact several sources agree on is a picture, a single-source track is a question.</p>

 <h2 id="recap">Key points</h2>
 <ul class="recap">
  <li>SA is assembled from every source and kept current; priorities set before flight protect it. <a href="#priorities">Priorities and what SA is</a></li>
  <li>Lookout is time-shared: near rocks, far rocks, check six first, and drop and rebuild sectors in order. <a href="#lookout">Lookout and the cross-check</a></li>
  <li>Mutual support is a briefed contract within the flight, and the controller shares the picture. <a href="#mutual">Mutual support and the team</a></li>
  <li>Goggles need some light; FLIR needs heat contrast and dry air; both narrow attention. <a href="#eoir">Infrared, pods and night vision</a></li>
  <li>A datalink shares others' tracks in a common picture, not your own confirmation. <a href="#datalink">Datalinks and the shared picture</a></li>
  <li>A correct Mode 4 reply identifies a friend; no reply leaves a track unknown. <a href="#iff">IFF: identifying friends</a></li>
  <li>Correlate sources before you act on a track. <a href="#fusion">Fusing the sources</a></li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="roles">Flight roles</h2>
 <p>The flight lead assigns each flight member's responsibilities, so each has a manageable number of tasks. <a href="#source-mch">{m('22')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Four-ship roles" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Responsibilities by position in a four-ship <a href="#source-mch">{m('22')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Position</th><th scope="col">Responsibilities</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Number one</th><td>Primary planner and decision maker, primary navigation and radar lookout, visual lookout for number two, primary engaged fighter if practical</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Number two</th><td>Formation position, visual lookout, mutual support of number one; navigation and radar awareness as other tasks allow</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Number three</th><td>Supports number one: secondary planner and alternate decision maker, support position for the lead element, secondary navigation and radar monitor, visual lookout for number four, mutual support of the whole flight, secondary engaged fighter if practical</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Number four</th><td>Formation position, visual lookout for the flight, mutual support of number three, navigation and radar awareness as other tasks allow</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Wingmen also help plan the mission, back up the navigation and have visual lookout and radar responsibilities of their own. <a href="#source-mch">{m('11')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="imaging">How well an imaging sensor sees</h2>
 <p>What you can tell from an image is described in levels, the <Term>Johnson criteria</Term>, first defined at the Army's Night Vision Laboratory: detection (something is there), orientation (its long axis can be seen), recognition (its class) and identification (its type within the class). The better the sensor, the higher the level at a given range. <a href="#source-usna">{u('10.5')}</a> A FLIR's two measures, thermal and angular resolution, trade against each other through detector size: one system cannot have the best of both. <a href="#source-usna">{u('10.5.3')}</a></p>

 <section class="sim-panel" aria-labelledby="sims">
  <h2 id="sims">In the sim</h2>
  <p>DCS World and Falcon BMS both model the F-16C's datalink, IFF interrogator and targeting pod, and BMS models goggles. This is what their official manuals say; helicopter sensors and goggles in either sim were not checked for this revision.</p>
  <h3>DCS: F-16C Viper</h3>
  <ul>
   <li>The F-16C's datalink is MIDS on the tactical network, sharing a common tactical picture with flight members (up to 8 including you), donors in the package (up to 4) and command and control platforms on their own mission channel. <a href="#source-f16">{d('452 to 453')}</a></li>
   <li>The F-16C's interrogator can challenge in Modes 1 to 4 in the real jet, but only Mode 4 is modeled: a short press of TMS left scans ±60° about the nose, a long press ±15° about the radar cursor, out to 115 NM. A correct reply shows on the radar display as a green circle with a "4" for two seconds, and may sit off its radar target, because IFF is less precise than the radar. <a href="#source-f16">{d('433 to 435')}</a></li>
   <li>The manual says what doctrine says: a valid Mode 4 reply confirms a friend, but no reply does not confirm a hostile; it may be a neutral or civil aircraft, and type, controller and datalink information, flight profile and point of origin all count. <a href="#source-f16">{d('435')}</a> The jet's own interrogator does not feed the track file; a Mode 4 status reaches it only over the datalink, from a platform such as AWACS. <a href="#source-f16">{d('470 to 471')}</a> The transponder's settings page is not implemented. <a href="#source-f16">{d('104 to 105')}</a></li>
   <li>The targeting pod carries a FLIR, usable day or night with wide and narrow fields of view, and a daylight-only TV camera with more magnification; picture-in-picture puts the TV image inside the wider FLIR view for awareness around it. <a href="#source-f16">{d('487')}</a></li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Falcon BMS 4.38</h3>
  <ul>
   <li>Training mission 17C teaches the improved data modem and Link 16, including using Link 16 information to build SA. <a href="#source-bms">{b('277')}</a></li>
   <li>Training mission 17B is IFF: the transponder and interrogator pages, scan and line-of-sight interrogation in Modes 1 to 4, and codes loaded from the mission's IFF policy through the data cartridge. A correct reply is a green circle; an incomplete one is a yellow square, treated as unknown. <a href="#source-bms">{b('266 to 270')}</a></li>
   <li>BMS goggles toggle on a key and deliberately narrow your field of view, to simulate tunnel vision. <a href="#source-bms">{b('136')}</a></li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Where the sims and the sources differ, or say nothing</h3>
  <ul>
   <li>BMS's mission 17A has you treat contacts as hostile after a negative Mode 4 reply, under that mission's rules of engagement, and in DCS a data cartridge rule can mark a no-reply datalink track hostile. Doctrine treats no reply as unknown, so in a sim it is the mission's rules, not IFF, that make it hostile. <a href="#source-bms">{b('261, 265')}</a> <a href="#source-f16">{d('471 to 472')}</a></li>
   <li>BMS's manual says Mode 3/A carries altitude; in the mode table above, altitude is Mode C and 3/A is the code. <a href="#source-bms">{b('267')}</a></li>
   <li>Neither manual teaches a lookout time-share or a mutual support contract; on a monitor your view is limited by the screen, which the handbook's sector plan does not consider.</li>
   <li>BMS's goggle model narrows the view, as the source's 40° field of view does <a href="#source-wiki-nvd">[6, Night-vision device]</a>; whether either sim models the need for some light, or the loss of detail, was not checked.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>To practise:</strong> fly a sortie with the datalink display and the radar both up, and interrogate, and for each track say which sources hold it and whether it answered before you act: Figure 1's question.</p>
 </section>

