 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> In close formation in cloud, what moves up the priority list?</summary><p>Avoiding the other aircraft in your flight: the handbook puts it above the ground in that case.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> After a hard turn you had only the near rocks in your cross-check. What next?</summary><p>Rebuild one sector at a time: far rocks, check six, then the rest.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Your radar holds a group the controller has not called. What do you do?</summary><p>Call it in the format in use and ask for a declaration; the controller repeats it with a name and a declaration if known.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Why might goggles show little on a moonless, overcast night?</summary><p>An intensifier needs some light from stars, moon or sky; with none, only an infrared sensor sees.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> A linked track shows a formation as one symbol. Why?</summary><p>Formation tracking: one track with a strength field stands for a formation that keeps a steady relationship, to cut workload and messages.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Only the datalink holds a track to the east, with no identity. What is it in your picture?</summary><p>An unknown: keep it, and ask about it, until another source correlates or identifies it.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 7</span> Your Mode 4 challenge gets no reply from a contact. Can you call it hostile?</summary><p>Not on IFF alone. No reply means only that it is not identified as a friend: it may carry no transponder, or be neutral. A hostile identification needs other means, such as non-cooperative target recognition, electronic support, a visual identification or procedural identification under the theater's criteria, as the rules of engagement require.</p></details>

